WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.580
MASS LAYOFFS:&nbsp;

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.220
By now, you’ve probably heard about the mass&nbsp;
layoffs at the high flying tech companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:33.420
Stripe cut 1,100 jobs, Coinbase cut 1,100 jobs,&nbsp;
Shopify cut 1,000 jobs, Snapchat cut 1,000 jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.240
Robinhood laid off a third of their staff, Tesla&nbsp;
laid of a tenth of their salaried staff, and Meta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:45.660
laid off a whopping 11,000 employees. And those&nbsp;
are just the companies that have already resorted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:50.160
to layoffs. While the remaining big tech companies&nbsp;
haven’t started laying off many people yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:57.120
they have pulled back on hiring substantially.&nbsp;
Apple, Google, Microosft, Uber, Lyft, Nvidia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:03.360
Intel, and many more all have hiring freezes&nbsp;
in place. Now, you can’t feel too bad for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.220
these employees because it’s not the same as&nbsp;
blue collar workers being laid off. Most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.560
these guy were earning high $200s if not more and&nbsp;
were on track to becoming deca millionaires. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.660
it’s not like they’ll be struggling to get by.&nbsp;
What may be difficult, however, is to score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.640
another job with the same compensation. With all&nbsp;
of silicon valley pulling back, many of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:28.800
guys will be forced to find jobs at background&nbsp;
companies like AT&amp;T or Texas Instruments. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.760
these companies will still pay generously in&nbsp;
relation to what the average American earns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.260
their total compensations will be nowhere&nbsp;
near what silicon valley offered. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:44.700
the highest paid software engineers at AT&amp;T earn&nbsp;
$145,000 per year which is not a bad amount, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:50.760
the highest paid software engineers at Facebook&nbsp;
earn $1.82 million per year. Even the entry level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:57.660
at Facebook is $23,000 higher that the best level&nbsp;
at AT&amp;T. Also, these background companies will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.620
probably be hesitant when it comes to extending&nbsp;
job offers to these laid off FAANG employees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:07.320
not because they’re not qualified, but because&nbsp;
they definitely wont be loyal. Everyone knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.540
that these guys will switch back to silicon&nbsp;
valley as soon as its viable, so it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:17.640
make a whole lot of sense to hire someone that&nbsp;
you know will probably be gone in 2 years. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.860
the main reason for this is simply how absurd&nbsp;
silicon valley compensations have gotten. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.538
how did these compensations get so high and will&nbsp;
we see FAANG ever return to paying these salaries.

00:02:28.538 --> 00:02:34.860
THE MECHANICS OF TECH SALARIES:
At first glance, it’s easy to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:40.080
off FAANG’s massive salaries as result of their&nbsp;
profitability. These companies often have profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.240
margins of 30 or 40%, so they’re able to pay&nbsp;
rather high salaries and still make a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:50.280
of money. And while this definitely does play&nbsp;
a role in FAANG compensation, it’s by far the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.320
smallest factor. The main factor allowing for&nbsp;
such compensation is actually the exponential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:00.480
growth of these companies. If you take a closer&nbsp;
look at FAANG compensation, you’ll see that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:06.000
vast majority comes from stock based compensation,&nbsp;
and that their base salaries aren’t actually all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.300
that crazy. Take product managers at Facebook&nbsp;
for example. Product directors get paid $1.34&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:16.140
million per year, but only $325,000 of that is&nbsp;
actually base salary. Product directors at FAANG&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:21.180
are equivalent to vice presidents at non FAANG&nbsp;
companies like Exxon. And you wouldn’t be that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:27.240
surprised to hear that a Exxon VP is making $300&nbsp;
to $500,000 per year. So, if we’re just looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:32.640
base salaries, FAANG companies aren’t actually all&nbsp;
that different from non FAANG companies. The real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:39.720
difference is that on top of this, you get paid&nbsp;
$898,000 in stock at Facebook. Now, there’s a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:44.520
good reasons that FAANG does this. First of all,&nbsp;
if your employees own stock, they’ll be far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.620
motivated to help the company make more money. And&nbsp;
secondly, stock compensation generally comes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.940
vesting periods of 4 years meaning that employees&nbsp;
have to stick around for at least 4 years to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:59.760
redeem all of their stock. So, this kind of locks&nbsp;
them in for a bit. Insiders like to call this the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.560
golden handcuffs. But, the most important reason&nbsp;
that so much of the compensation comes from stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.360
is because that’s the only way these companies&nbsp;
can afford to pay out such salaries. While these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:15.000
companies do make a bunch of money, even they&nbsp;
wouldn’t want to pay these salaries in cash. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.340
wait a minute, isn’t stock compensation the same&nbsp;
thing? Don’t companies have to pay for the stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:26.100
they award their employees? Well, yes they do,&nbsp;
but not in the way that you might think. Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:31.020
doesn’t go onto the stock market and buy $900,000&nbsp;
worth of stock so that they can pay a director.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:37.380
Instead, they simply dilute existing shareholders&nbsp;
and print new stock. Facebook, for example, had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.820
87,000 employees before the layoffs. If we&nbsp;
assume that the average Facebook employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:47.340
gets a $100,000 worth of stock every year, that&nbsp;
works out to a total stock compensation of $8.7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.860
billion per year. If Facebook paid this is cash,&nbsp;
that would wipe out a third of their net income,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.780
but when they were worth a trillion dollars,&nbsp;
this compensation was equivalent to less than 1%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:04.020
shareholder dilution. I think you can see why the&nbsp;
latter is far preferred. The only caveat though is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.520
that these companies have to continue growing&nbsp;
exponentially. If these companies start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:14.520
stagnate or worse start to sell off, shareholders&nbsp;
will no longer be ok with dilution. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:20.100
why stagnant companies like Exxon and AT&amp;T can’t&nbsp;
compete with FAANG in terms of total compensation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:25.020
While they’re able to come close in terms of&nbsp;
cash compensation, they simply can’t afford the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.880
same dilution. And speaking of dilution, with&nbsp;
how the stocks of high flying companies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.800
been performing, it’s not clear if silicon valley&nbsp;
can afford such dilution moving forward either.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.320
SHIFTING LANDSCAPES:
It’s no secret that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.280
stock market has been getting destroyed, and&nbsp;
most of this destruction has centered around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:52.380
silicon valley. Affirm is down 90%, Robinhood is&nbsp;
down 80%, Lyft is down 83%, Netflix is down 58%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:59.100
and even Facebook is down 75%. As these companies&nbsp;
sell off, stock compensation related dilution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:04.800
becomes more and more expensive especially for&nbsp;
new hires. Remember the $8.7 billion that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.020
calculated for Facebook? At a trillion dollar&nbsp;
valuation, that’s less that 1% dilution, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:16.080
at their current $300 billion valuation, that’s&nbsp;
closer to 3% dilution. And where this becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.720
really dangerous is if Facebook doesn’t grow for&nbsp;
the next 10 years. From an outside perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:29.160
this wouldn’t be that bad. After all, $300 billion&nbsp;
is still a ginormous valuation and Facebook would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:34.260
probably still be making plenty of money. But,&nbsp;
from a shareholder perspective, this would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:41.100
a disaster. Here’s the thing, 3% dilution for 10&nbsp;
years works out to 30% total dilution. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:46.260
that if you had a $100,000 stake in Facebook, it&nbsp;
would be worth $70,000 by the end of 10 years even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:51.840
though the company itself stayed stable. Factor in&nbsp;
inflation and you’re initial $100,000 stake is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:57.540
worth closer to just $50,000. I think you can see&nbsp;
why investors aren’t exactly thrilled to pick up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:03.540
these companies even though they’re down so much.&nbsp;
And honestly, looking forward, it doesn’t look too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:09.300
good for any of the silicon valley giants. Apple&nbsp;
iPhone sales peaked years ago. This is why Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:14.640
stopped reporting how many iPhones they sold and&nbsp;
started increasing prices aggressively. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:19.380
able to continue growing profits thanks to these&nbsp;
price increase along with services revenue. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:23.820
it looks like the upside from these are starting&nbsp;
to run out, not because Apple is doing anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:29.160
wrong, but simply because they’ve reached market&nbsp;
saturation. And it looks like it’s the same case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:34.680
with all of their peers as well. Out of the 4.72&nbsp;
billion people who have access to the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:41.220
4.3 billion already use Google. Similarly,&nbsp;
Instagram is about to reach 2.5 billion monthly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:46.440
active users and WhatsApp is at 2.2 billion&nbsp;
users. How many more people could realistically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:53.760
use Google, YouTube, Netflix, iCloud, and Uber?&nbsp;
The answer is not that many. All these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.560
are essentially mega monopolies that have&nbsp;
already captured the entire market. And with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.000
advertisers pulling back due to the recession,&nbsp;
these companies will make less money even with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.940
a stable user count atleast until the end of&nbsp;
the recession. And there’s no question that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:10.320
this will result in these companies rethinking&nbsp;
just how much they’re paying their employees.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:15.960
A CROSSROADS:
Alright, so here’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:21.540
what we’ve established. The only reason that tech&nbsp;
companies are able to pay so much is because most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:26.820
of the compensation is in the form of stock which&nbsp;
is paid for by stock dilution. This, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:31.620
only works when the companies’ stocks are booming.&nbsp;
With these companies reaching market saturation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:36.660
along with the economic uncertainty, it’s not&nbsp;
clear how much longer these companies can pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:42.720
these insane salaries, and that brings us to the&nbsp;
crossroads that we’re at today. These companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.580
really only have two options with the first being&nbsp;
pivoting to a new industry with plenty of growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:53.160
left. The company that’s most iconic for trying&nbsp;
to do this is of course Facebook. They changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:57.900
their company’s name to Meta and they’re betting&nbsp;
it all on the Metaverse. They’re literally looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:02.820
to spend a $100 billion on this. And while&nbsp;
they’ve received a lot of criticism for this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.720
there’s no question that if they are able&nbsp;
to figure it out in the way that they’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:12.240
envisioned as opposed to how it is right now, it&nbsp;
would truly be huge. This would likely be enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:18.000
for Meta to grow to a multi trillion market cap&nbsp;
if not over $10 trillion, allowing them to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:22.860
their stock compensation even more ludicrous.&nbsp;
Another company who’s betting it all is Netflix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:27.720
Netflix is turning around and spending all&nbsp;
of their earnings on in house productions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:34.620
hoping that this will make them more independent&nbsp;
of production studios. They literally spent $270&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:42.120
million on the most recent season of Stranger&nbsp;
Things. Investors are obviously scared of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:46.320
massive pivots and that’s why these stocks&nbsp;
are the most beat down. On the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.820
some other tech giants are choosing to play it&nbsp;
safe and are slowly becoming dividend stocks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:56.940
Likely the two best examples of this are Apple&nbsp;
and Microsoft. Unlike Google, Facebook, Netflix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:02.880
and Amazon, Apple and Microsoft already pay&nbsp;
dividends. And it looks like they’re simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:09.000
going to lean more into this. These companies were&nbsp;
already on the lower end of FAANG compensation. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:15.360
Apple, for example, even the top level software&nbsp;
engineers were “only” getting paid $677,000 per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.400
year which is almost 3 times less than the top&nbsp;
engineers at Facebook. As these companies choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:25.020
different paths moving forward, it appears&nbsp;
that this discrepancy will only grow larger.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:28.920
THE FUTURE OF TECH COMPENSATIONS:&nbsp;

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:33.360
In the end, with all of these tech layoffs,&nbsp;
it appears that the ball if finally shifting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.740
from the employees’ side to the employers’&nbsp;
side. Thanks to the seemingly unlimited VC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:42.240
funding and hypergrowth associated with these&nbsp;
companies, employees have been able to negotiate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.880
salaries as high as 7 figures. But, with the&nbsp;
economic downturn and a bunch of newly laid of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:51.720
tech workers looking for work, it doesn’t&nbsp;
seem like these numbers will fly anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:57.540
atleast not for some time. In the meantime, these&nbsp;
guys will probably have to take a substantial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:02.820
paycut and join a background company or maybe even&nbsp;
start their own company. On the brightside though,&nbsp;

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:07.800
I doubt that the recession is going to wipe out&nbsp;
any of these big tech names. Eventually, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:13.260
will get to the otherside and start hiring again,&nbsp;
but when they do, the compensations will probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:18.300
look a lot different. Companies that are choosing&nbsp;
stability like Apple and Microsoft will likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:23.580
be far less generous when it comes to handing out&nbsp;
stock. If they’re embracing dividends, they can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:28.800
also embrace growth and dilution. On the other&nbsp;
hand, companies like Facebook and startups like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.840
Coinbase will likely hand out more stock&nbsp;
compensation than ever, but that of course comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.460
along with a bunch of additional risk. You’re&nbsp;
essentially betting that the metaverse or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:44.160
product the company is pivoting will actually&nbsp;
pan out. So, it appears that the days of low risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:49.620
high compensation are quickly coming to an end,&nbsp;
but that’s just what I think. Do you think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:54.480
Metaverse will pan out? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you wish FAANG salaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:58.680
would become the norm. And of course, consider&nbsp;
